<section class="faq">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <h3>How deep should soil be in a raised garden bed?</h3>
    <p>Most vegetables require 8 to 12 inches of quality soil depth for healthy root development. Deep taproot crops like carrots and tomatoes benefit from 12 to 18 inches of rich soil depth.</p>

    <h3>How many bags of soil are in a cubic yard?</h3>
    <p>One cubic yard equals 27 cubic feet. That converts to 36 bags of 0.75 cu ft topsoil or 27 bags of 1.0 cu ft garden soil.</p>

    <h3>What is the difference between topsoil, garden soil, and potting mix?</h3>
    <p>Topsoil is raw screened earth used for filling deep holes and grading lawns. Garden soil is topsoil enriched with organic compost for ground garden beds. Potting mix is lightweight soil-free medium (peat moss, perlite, bark) designed for container drainage.</p>

    <h3>How much does soil settle after watering?</h3>
    <p>Freshly filled raised beds settle by approximately 10 to 15 percent after thorough watering. Filling beds slightly above the rim accounts for natural soil compaction.</p>
